# Line endings are a correctness concern here, not a preference.
#
# The specification requires LF for canonical `.vib` and `.vibon` output, and
# the conformance corpus compares formatter results byte for byte. Without
# these rules a Windows checkout rewrites every file on the way in and the
# formatter check fails, or worse, a fixture is silently altered.

# Normalize on commit and check out LF on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Conformance fixtures are the exception: their bytes are the test. Some
# reader cases deliberately carry CRLF, a missing trailing newline, or other
# byte-exact shapes, so Git must never normalize them. `eol` must be
# unspecified as well as `text` unset, because a set `eol` converts on its own
# and would otherwise be inherited from the rule above. `diff` keeps these
# files readable in review despite `-text`.
*.vib -text !eol diff
*.vibon -text !eol diff
